  "projects": [
    {
      "title": "Hug Chair",
      "category": "Furniture",
      "collaborator": "Xiyao Min",
      "status": "Finished",
      "specs": "Material - Oak\nYear - 2025",
      "description": "From 13 April to 13 October 2025, representatives from countries across the globe gathered to showcase innovation, culture, and sustainability at the World Expo in Osaka, Japan. For Switzerland’s pavilion, MA Product Design students at ECAL were tasked with designing a stackable wooden chair to be produced in Japan by Karimoku New Standard, intended to furnish the light-weight, bubble-inspired architecture by Manuel Herz.\n\nHUG is a circular chair that stacks inversely a feature that not only informs its name but also visually embodies the essence of collaboration. This versatile design accommodates a range of uses, from conferences to general pavilion seating.",
      "images": [
        "/images/iYbzydTvurBJfpsK1n3KFI8wv80.avif",
        "/images/dlJtuN9IG2S3n3HpTVbWEo5bSFQ.avif",
        "/images/wc4eK6kwMuafsajgsmwvlHaf4.avif",
        "/images/kxRSTjMYwDAn6kgce1gU9E1Vefw.avif",
        "/images/4MTfTLHWCVVmheULB9APw7bekAM.jpg",
        "/images/YV60eNQFu6Rg1xOaEIfzDWTE.webp",
        "/images/Vfx3kbYIrhxPb1qjmm9tW8LXBI.avif"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "JK2 Synthesizer",
      "category": "Product",
      "collaborator": "Jahn Koutrios",
      "status": "Completed",
      "specs": "Material - Aluminium, Beach\nYear - 2024/2025",
      "description": "As a longtime synth-nerd and lover of all things music related, I’ve always dreamed of designing and building my own analog synthesizer. The journey began with defining the sound I wanted to create. Drawing inspiration from 1970s Minimoog schematics, I added a few modern touches to make it my own. From there, I designed and ordered custom PCBs, hand-soldered over 1200 components, and built a case focused for domestic spaces; combining aluminum and beech wood, I aimed for a synth that’s both easily transportable and visually balanced. Rugged yet refined.",
      "images": [
        "/images/ovbUZQVtmAoGQoWajy6rLokdaA.avif",
        "/images/Spotless Brushed Aluminium Synthesizer.png",
        "/images/5Ho1GQRXIeNvad876WCKbsclBAA.jpg",
        "/images/jFSNc4z8qTRHemkrAkaShjx9Imc.avif",
        "/images/2MDPNEcCwkj4OYiC3NbCvSnpNt4.avif",
        "/images/dsc7129.avif",
        "/images/TPKLgTgHlj5atG1Vsv8dvwVQA.webp",
        "/images/dsc7135.avif"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Lasso",
      "category": "Material development",
      "status": "Researching",
      "specs": "Material - Nylon\nYear - 2024/2025",
      "description": "Lasso is a research project focused on repurposing discarded nylon climbing rope once it is deemed unsafe. Driven by an interest in high-performance materials, this exploration transforms soft rope into a rigid structure. By applying heat at 240–260°C, the fibers fuse together, creating a hardened plastic that can be shaped into furniture. From this principle, a series of 3 stools were created to showcase the potential of this technique.",
      "images": [
        "/images/jMLw6rgjiQVA16jBbCYDMqvPyk-1.jpg",
        "/images/vDZikIHJeC9fJcEOwcwpNZfLlU.webp",
        "/images/ImUFJvkWmpGOgyJ46Al3uQa8Iew.avif",
        "/images/R1lR761Znt2HRYEiofQX8izdeDk.avif",
        "/images/1hqSHg7FpE2xEpeGQ8K5WEkkn8w.avif",
        "/images/KXcqB4qZTaYwVImZ6J555YhDz8.webp",
        "/images/ljKw0phonKve4IbdnRg0THWcMlc.webp"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Loader 421",
      "category": "Transportation",
      "collaborator": "SIMMER",
      "status": "Completed",
      "specs": "Material - Aluminium, EPP, Rubber\nYear - 2022/2023",
      "description": "Ecoride is a Swedish electric bicycle company founded in 2008 with a mission to make quality e-bikes accessible and affordable. As they’ve expanded across Europe, the demand for more distinctive and versatile models has grown. They approached us to design their newest two-wheeled cargo bike; envisioned as a true alternative to the car for urban families across Europe.\n\nOur concept focused on safety and comfort, featuring an intuitive safety bar for them to hold onto, an easy-access step for climbing in, and integrated reflectors positioned similarly to those on a car. All this whilst maintaining the familiar handling and feel of a traditional bicycle.",
      "images": [
        "/images/abler1ffUW946sctkvVtLRT9MA.avif",
        "/images/Io4PTYPn8eYx0MjaozlHgbMuuRE.webp",
        "/images/tAIiXWW5CbjLDMW98YfVqPwfzw.avif",
        "/images/FZEl2AslgbRkfpXpgdgKPxrgshE.avif",
        "/images/uI5iBq1sIihDOGcq8PGxrqo0lo.avif"
      ],
      "credit": "Robert During Jansson / SIMMER"
    },
    {
      "title": "Laff Machine 1",
      "category": "Experimental",
      "collaborator": "Helsinki Design Week 2025",
      "status": "Exhibited",
      "specs": "Material - Aluminium\nYear - 2025",
      "description": "Inspired by the original 1950s Laff Box by Charles Douglass, the LM-1 is a sample-based synthesizer capable to layering 60 different laughter samples. Up to six voices can be played simultaneously, allowing users to build complex textures of laughter - using everything from subtle chuckles to the sound of a large, laughing audience. \n\nThe LM-1 was first exhibited as a commissioned piece for the 'Happiness, Can it be designed?' exhibition curated by Anniina Koivu for the Helsinki Design Week 2025. ",
      "images": [
        "/images/JtuZgoZq4hdbRNxWiyASqUMOPDw-1.avif",
        "/images/YDLTlNobB2qMgk18YsqmF7iuQ.avif",
        "/images/FeIU1LRnKpikut8HB0Jb15dTKo.avif",
        "/images/tKpMtNGQSBsqPPer5pTaKTvzMQ.avif",
        "/images/ZBuMhK3eKut3QsXU6pdzuyKYNj0.avif",
        "/images/ff32e043-425f-498d-a47e-217a24d6b0d2.jpeg"
      ],
      "credit": "Photo Credit: Justus Hirvi "
    },
    {
      "title": "Tornet",
      "category": "Product",
      "collaborator": "Viessmann",
      "status": "Finished",
      "specs": "Material - Steel Mesh\nYear - 2023",
      "description": "Viessmann tasked us to create the heatpump for the future. The concept of Tornet is to use metal mesh gabions to make it nearly flat-packable, where the customer could use local materials to fill it up with whatever material is most easily accessed. Not only would this ensure a sustainable heatpump, which uses a lot less metal and plastic compared to current models, it also blends into its environment. A variety of materials were tested to find the optimal grid, which resulted in a circular mesh tower with easy access to all its important components.",
      "images": [
        "/images/big-render.avif",
        "/images/Hk2EAPlK1IDLHRcghO77eLddJYs-2.avif",
        "/images/final-hero-4.avif",
        "/images/dsc06365.avif",
        "/images/dsc06355.avif"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "about": {
    "text": "Jacob Kouthoofd Mårtensson is an industrial designer exploring how small, creative solutions can spark meaningful impact. With a background in Art Direction and a Master’s in Product Design from [ECAL](https://ecal.ch), this intersection allows him to craft iconic silhouettes, iterate quickly, communicate ideas clearly, and embrace the creative freedom to abandon what doesn’t work.\n\nHe has contributed to projects with studios such as Form Us With Love, Pentagram London, and SIMMER, for clients including Volvo, H&M, IKEA, and Disney. Currently working for Reed Kram AB. ",
    "portrait": "/images/PORTRAIT OFME.jpg",
